## Step 2: Unpowered Flight (Testing Lift with Kites)
Before attempting complex machines, you must test your materials and wing designs using unpowered, tethered devices.

   1. The Airframe: Use your lathe to shave down light, flexible woods (like bamboo or willow) into thin, skeletal spars.
   2. The Membrane: Cover the wooden frame with a lightweight, airtight skin. As a lone survivor, your best options are tightly woven wild silk, beaten tree-bark cloth (tapa), or dried, stretched animal bladders sealed with pine resin glue.
   3. The Airfoil Shape: Curve the top of the wing structure slightly while keeping the bottom flat. When air hits the front edge, it moves faster over the curved top than the flat bottom. This creates lower air pressure on top, generating lift (Bernoulli's principle). Tether this to the ground with your high-tensile rope to observe how different wing shapes handle wind gusts.

------------------------------
## Step 3: Rotational Flight (The Air Screw)
To transition from passive wind-catching to active thrust generation, you can use your lathe to carve a rotary wing, heavily inspired by Leonardo da Vinci’s aerial screw or a modern propeller.

   1. Carving the Propeller: Mount a light blocks of wood on your lathe. Carefully carve two matching blades angled at roughly 15 to 20 degrees. This creates a rotating wing.
   2. Testing Torque: Wrap a string around a central vertical shaft attached to your propeller. Pulling the string rapidly will spin the blades, forcing air downward and causing the small wooden rotor to lift briefly into the air. This demonstrates how rotational mechanical energy translates into vertical thrust.

------------------------------
## The Ultimate Bottleneck: Power-to-Weight Ratio
While you can build lightweight gliders or small propeller toys using primitive materials, true sustained or automated flying agents require a continuous, lightweight power source.
Human muscles are too heavy for the amount of power they produce, and steam engines (made of heavy iron and boiling water) are far too massive to lift themselves off the ground. To advance past basic gliders, your technology tree must first detour into the electrical and chemical eras to discover how to refine petroleum for internal combustion engines or build lightweight batteries and electric motors.
Would you like to step backward into the industrial tree to explore how to generate electricity using copper wire and magnets, or would you prefer to dive into the mathematics and geometry of carving a perfect airfoil wing on your lathe?
